56:**That second kind surfaced only when the first was discharged**: with a codex marker present
57:each stage read HELD and was accepted, and removing it revealed the true count. *One held
58:marker can mask another gap* -- section H cannot see a shortfall that a different hold covers.
59:
60:**Neither kind is a blank.** A blank cell and a seat failure must not look alike, and an
61:allocation is a third thing again: recorded by seat name, per row, with the reason.
62:
63:**This is ONE outage plus one allocation, not 38 decisions, and it is written once rather than once per panel.**
64:**THE CODEX HISTORY, kept because it calibrates what a wall looks like.** Across ten panels it
65:produced the identical signature every time -- the echoed brief followed by the usage-limit
66:error -- in files of 26,992 / 11,180 / 10,036 / 8,323 / 12,414 / 11,938 / 10,726 / 16,694 /
67:19,649 / 21,348 bytes. *A size check alone scores those as the largest answers in their panels.*
68:`panel.sh`'s seat check caught each as RATE-LIMITED rather than counting the blank as agreement,
69:and the discharge re-verified every rerun the same way: delta over the brief, and the absence of
70:the usage-limit string in the tail.
71:
72:**A DISCHARGED CODEX FINDING IS HISTORICAL UNLESS RE-CHECKED.** Each rerun answered the brief AS
73:IT WAS FIRED -- byte-identical, so its answer is comparable with the seats that answered live --
74:and the tree has moved since. Measured example: its rtcdet verdict was DEFECTIVE on a `+1`
75:mutant that "admits UINT64_MAX while all nine rows remain green" -- correct against the 9-row
76:table it was shown, and MEASURED CLOSED against today's 18-row table. Three further codex
77:recommendations are already settled by measurements on the record (`tfreq` is fixed;
78:`mrwstore2`'s "produce a real-guest witness" was measured impossible; `constblind`'s drop was
79:rejected after a live instance was found).
